ID<code>argon:carry</code> While enabled, grabs the living entity you look at and holds it on a damped spring at an attachment point. It lags and swings as you move but never goes past "maximum_distance". Disabling lets go. SettingsExampleIn a power <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>range</code>DoubleHow far away, in blocks, a target can be grabbed from.✓/<code>attachment</code>Vector 3D,<code>right_shoulder</code><code>left_shoulder</code><code>right_hand</code><code>left_hand</code><code>chest</code><code>above_head</code><code>main_hand</code><code>off_hand</code><code>main_shoulder</code><code>off_shoulder</code>Where the carried entity is drawn toward, in the wearer's frame (x right, y up from the feet, z forward): an [x, y, z] offset, or right_shoulder, left_shoulder, right_hand, left_hand, chest, above_head, or main/off_hand and main/off_shoulder by the wearer's main arm.✗right_shoulder<code>preferred_distance</code>DoubleRadius around the attachment point inside which the target is held only loosely.✗0.6<code>maximum_distance</code>DoubleRadius around the attachment point the target can never leave (a wall can still push it past this).✗1.5<code>stiffness</code>DoubleSpring strength pulling the target to the attachment point.✗0.2<code>damping</code>DoubleHow strongly the target's motion relative to you is resisted. Higher settles faster, lower swings longer.✗0.5<code>gravity_influence</code>DoubleHow much normal gravity acts on the target, so it hangs below you when not pulled up. 0 is none, 1 is full.✗1.0<code>inertia</code>DoubleThe target's mass. Spring and damping are divided by it, so higher means more lag and swing.✗1.0<code>follow_strength</code>DoubleHow much of each change in your velocity is passed straight to the target. 0 means only the spring moves it, 1 means it follows exactly.✗0.35<code>acquire_ticks</code>IntegerMost ticks the initial pull-in lasts before normal carry physics takes over.✗6<code>target_collision</code>BooleanWhether the carried entity is kept out of walls. If a block is in the way it is pulled back along the line from you instead of going through it.✗true<code>preserve_release_velocity</code>BooleanWhether the target keeps its velocity when let go, rather than stopping dead.✗true<code>allow_mobs</code>BooleanWhether non-player entities can be grabbed.✗true<code>allow_players</code>BooleanWhether players can be grabbed.✗false<code>visual_tilt</code>DoubleHow much the carried entity pitches and rolls with the sway when drawn. 0 keeps it upright, 1 is as calculated, higher exaggerates, negative tilts the other way.✗1.0<code>arm_pose</code>BooleanWhether your arm on the attachment side aims at the carried entity automatically (both arms for a centered attachment). Turn it off to animate the arms yourself with the argon.carry_* queries.✗true<code>safe_release</code>BooleanWhether the released entity takes no fall damage from its next fall.✗false<code>excluded_tags</code>Entity Type ID(s) / TagEntity types in any of these tags can never be grabbed, for example #c:bosses.✗/<code>max_width</code>DoubleEntities wider than this many blocks can't be grabbed. 0 means no limit.✗0.0<code>max_height</code>DoubleEntities taller than this many blocks can't be grabbed. 0 means no limit.✗0.0<code>max_health</code>DoubleEntities with more max health than this can't be grabbed. 0 means no limit.✗0.0<code>max_duration</code>IntegerThe carry lets go on its own after this many ticks. 0 means no limit.✗0<code>player_duration_multiplier</code>DoubleMultiplies max_duration when the carried entity is a player, so players can be held for a shorter time.✗1.0</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:carry",
  "range": 3
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>{
  "name": "Example Power",
  "icon": "minecraft:command_block",
  "abilities": {
    "carry": {
      "type": "argon:carry",
      "range": 3
    }
  }
}
</code></pre>

