ID<code>argon:grapple</code> Shoots a rope-like beam out to a range and reels in what it hits. A hit living entity is pulled toward the caster, a hit block pulls the caster toward it. SettingsExampleIn a power <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>range</code>DoubleThe maximum distance the grapple can reach.✓/<code>attach_to_entity</code>BooleanIf true, hitting a living entity within range pulls it toward the caster.✗true<code>attach_to_block</code>BooleanIf true, hitting a block within range pulls the caster toward it.✗true<code>speed</code>DoubleThe velocity, in blocks/tick, that the pulled entity (or the caster) moves at.✓/<code>beam_renderer</code>IdentifierThe beam renderer id used to draw the rope (e.g. a custom one under data//palladium/beams/).✓/<code>effects</code>Beam Effect[]Mob effects applied to a living entity when the grapple hits it.✗/ <p><p><code>effects[]</code> (Beam Effect)</p></p> <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>effect</code>Mob Effect IDThe effect to apply.✓/<code>duration</code>Integer,Time String ("40s", "2m", etc.)How long the effect lasts.✗/<code>amplifier</code>Integer (0 ~ 255)The effect level, starting at 0.✗/<code>particles</code>BooleanWhether the effect shows its particles.✗/<code>size_tiers</code>Size Tier[]Changes or skips the effect depending on the width or type of the target.✗/</p> <p><p><code>effects[].size_tiers[]</code> (Size Tier)</p></p> <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>tag</code>Entity Type TagOnly entities in this tag use the tier.✗/<code>min_width</code>Double (0.0 ~ 64.0)The tier applies to entities at least this wide, in blocks.✗0.0<code>skip</code>BooleanIf true, matching entities do not get the effect at all.✗false<code>effect</code>Mob Effect IDReplaces the effect for matching entities.✗/<code>amplifier</code>Integer (0 ~ 255)Replaces the amplifier for matching entities.✗/<code>duration</code>Integer,Time String ("40s", "2m", etc.)Replaces the duration for matching entities.✗/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:grapple",
  "range": 20,
  "speed": 1.2,
  "beam_renderer": "example:beam_renderer_id"
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>{
  "name": "Example Power",
  "icon": "minecraft:command_block",
  "abilities": {
    "grapple": {
      "type": "argon:grapple",
      "range": 20,
      "speed": 1.2,
      "beam_renderer": "example:beam_renderer_id"
    }
  }
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:ground_beam</code> While enabled, draws damaging ground beams under entities with solid ground within "max_ground_depth" blocks. "mode" "area" (default) hits every other living entity within "range", "target" only the one you look at. SettingsExample 1Example 2In a power <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>beam_renderer</code>IdentifierThe id of the beam renderer to use for each ground-to-target beam.✓/<code>range</code>DoubleIn "area" mode, how far from the user, in blocks, to search for targets. In "target" mode, the farthest a target can be while you look at it.✓/<code>mode</code><code>area</code><code>target</code>Whether this affects every qualifying entity within "range" of the user ("area") or only the single qualifying entity the user is currently looking at ("target").✗area<code>max_ground_depth</code>DoubleHow far, in blocks, to search straight down from a target for solid ground. A target with no ground in that depth gets no beam and takes no damage that tick.✗64.0<code>damage</code>FloatDamage dealt to every beamed entity every "damage_rate" ticks. Leave at 0 for a cosmetic beam.✗0.0<code>damage_rate</code>Integer,Time String ("40s", "2m", etc.)How long, in ticks, to wait between damage applications.✗20<code>no_knockback</code>BooleanIf true, damaged entities are not knocked back.✗false<code>powers</code>Power Requirement,Power Requirement[]Restricts which entities can be targeted based on the powers they hold. Each entry lists a "power" (an explicit power id, a power tag ("#namespace:tag"), or a mix of both) and an optional "mode" - "include" (default) requires at least one matching power to be present, "exclude" requires all of them to be absent; an entity only counts if it satisfies every entry. Leave unset (the default) to target all living entities in range.✗/<code>damage_type</code>Damage Type IDA damage type ID such as "minecraft:magic" or one from a pack. Left unset, it is a player attack when a player uses this and a mob attack otherwise. Unknown types fall back to that.✗/</p> <p><p><code>powers</code> (Power Requirement)</p></p> <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>power</code>Power ID(s) / TagThe power or powers the entity has to have.✓/<code>mode</code><code>include</code><code>exclude</code>Whether the entity must have the power (include) or must not have it (exclude).✗include</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:ground_beam",
  "beam_renderer": "example:beam_renderer_id",
  "range": 10,
  "damage": 2,
  "no_knockback": true
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:ground_beam",
  "beam_renderer": "example:beam_renderer_id",
  "range": 10,
  "damage": 2,
  "powers": [
    {
      "power": "example:ally_power",
      "mode": "exclude"
    }
  ]
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>{
  "name": "Example Power",
  "icon": "minecraft:command_block",
  "abilities": {
    "ground_beam": {
      "type": "argon:ground_beam",
      "beam_renderer": "example:beam_renderer_id",
      "range": 10,
      "damage": 2,
      "no_knockback": true
    }
  }
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:hearing</code> Client only. Labels each sound you hear with its subtitle and pins it in the world until it fades. Sounds beyond their normal range are forwarded up to "range" blocks as silent markers. SettingsExampleIn a power <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>range</code>(Dynamic) ValueThe farthest a sound can be from the holder, in blocks, and still show. Includes sounds the server forwards from beyond their normal distance. Capped at 512 for forwarded sounds.✗32.0<code>duration</code>Integer (1 ~ 12000)How long a sound's marker lasts before it is gone. It stays fully visible for the first 60% and fades over the rest.✗100<code>max_markers</code>Integer (1 ~ 512)The most markers shown at once; the oldest are dropped first.✗64<code>include_unlabeled</code>BooleanWhether sounds with no subtitle in the game's sound definitions are shown too, labeled with the sound's own id.✗false<code>see_through</code>BooleanWhether markers are visible through blocks.✗true<code>ignore_own</code>BooleanWhether sounds made at the holder's own position (their footsteps, their hurt sound) are skipped.✗true<code>scale</code>Float (0.1 ~ 8.0)Size multiplier for the marker text.✗1.0<code>color</code>ColorThe text color, white by default.✗/<code>exclude_sources</code>string[]Sound categories that are never shown: any of master, music, record, weather, block, hostile, neutral, player, ambient, voice, ui. Sounds not placed in the world (interface sounds) are always skipped.✗[ "master", "music", "record", "ui" ]<code>muffle_footsteps</code>BooleanWhether footsteps on a soft surface can't be heard. A step is skipped when the block the walker stands in or on is one of the "muffling_blocks". The server applies the same rule to sounds it forwards from far away.✗true<code>muffling_blocks</code>Block ID(s) / TagThe blocks that muffle footsteps when "muffle_footsteps" is on, given as explicit block IDs, a tag ("#namespace:tag"), or a mix of both. Left unset, it is vanilla's "#minecraft:dampens_vibrations" tag, which is wool and carpets.✗/<code>hostile_color</code>ColorText color for sounds made by hostile mobs, instead of "color".✗/<code>player_color</code>ColorText color for sounds made by players, instead of "color".✗/<code>neutral_color</code>ColorText color for sounds made by neutral and passive mobs, instead of "color".✗/<code>prioritize</code>BooleanWhen more sounds are heard than "max_markers" allows, drop the least significant (ambient and block sounds first, then far-away ones) instead of the oldest. Hostile and player sounds rank highest.✗false<code>edge_arrows</code>BooleanShows a small pointer on the edge of the screen for every marker that is outside your view, pointing the way to the sound.✗false</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:hearing",
  "range": 48,
  "duration": 120,
  "color": "#FFFFFF",
  "exclude_sources": [
    "master",
    "music",
    "record",
    "ui"
  ]
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>{
  "name": "Example Power",
  "icon": "minecraft:command_block",
  "abilities": {
    "hearing": {
      "type": "argon:hearing",
      "range": 48,
      "duration": 120,
      "color": "#FFFFFF",
      "exclude_sources": [
        "master",
        "music",
        "record",
        "ui"
      ]
    }
  }
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:held_target</code> Deals damage, applies effects and knocks back the entity the user holds with argon:carry (or let go of in the last few ticks). Each entry runs on the first tick, every tick, or the last tick. Does nothing when nothing is held. SettingsExampleIn a power <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>applications</code>Held Application[]What to do to the held target. timing is "first_tick", "tick" or "last_tick"; interval is how often a "tick" entry runs; damage is a Value; armor_penetration is the fraction from 0 to 1 of the target's armor ignored by the damage; damage_type is a damage type ID (unset, a player attack); effects is a list of { effect*, duration, amplifier, particles }; knockback_distance (Value, blocks, away from the user) and lift (Value, upward speed) are weakened by knockback resistance; reset_invulnerability (default false) clears the target's hit immunity just before the damage so several quick hits all land.✓/<code>release_window</code>IntegerHow many ticks after the target was let go it still counts as held, so an entry can run on the tick the carry ends.✗6</p> <p><p><code>applications[]</code> (Held Application)</p></p> <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>timing</code><code>first_tick</code><code>tick</code><code>last_tick</code>When the application happens while the ability is held.✓/<code>interval</code>Integer,Time String ("40s", "2m", etc.)The delay between applications when the timing is tick.✗/<code>damage</code>(Dynamic) ValueThe damage dealt.✗/<code>armor_penetration</code>Float (0.0 ~ 1.0)How much of the target's armor is ignored.✗/<code>damage_type</code>Damage Type IDThe damage type used.✗/<code>effects</code>Beam Effect[]Effects applied to the target.✗/<code>knockback_distance</code>(Dynamic) ValueHow far the target is knocked back.✗/<code>lift</code>(Dynamic) ValueHow much the target is lifted.✗/<code>reset_invulnerability</code>BooleanWhether the target's invulnerability frames are reset so the hit lands.✗/</p> <p><p><code>applications[].effects[]</code> (Beam Effect)</p></p> <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>effect</code>Mob Effect IDThe effect to apply.✓/<code>duration</code>Integer,Time String ("40s", "2m", etc.)How long the effect lasts.✗/<code>amplifier</code>Integer (0 ~ 255)The effect level, starting at 0.✗/<code>particles</code>BooleanWhether the effect shows its particles.✗/<code>size_tiers</code>Size Tier[]Changes or skips the effect depending on the width or type of the target.✗/</p> <p><p><code>applications[].effects[].size_tiers[]</code> (Size Tier)</p></p> <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>tag</code>Entity Type TagOnly entities in this tag use the tier.✗/<code>min_width</code>Double (0.0 ~ 64.0)The tier applies to entities at least this wide, in blocks.✗0.0<code>skip</code>BooleanIf true, matching entities do not get the effect at all.✗false<code>effect</code>Mob Effect IDReplaces the effect for matching entities.✗/<code>amplifier</code>Integer (0 ~ 255)Replaces the amplifier for matching entities.✗/<code>duration</code>Integer,Time String ("40s", "2m", etc.)Replaces the duration for matching entities.✗/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:held_target",
  "applications": []
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>{
  "name": "Example Power",
  "icon": "minecraft:command_block",
  "abilities": {
    "held_target": {
      "type": "argon:held_target",
      "applications": []
    }
  }
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:highlight</code> Client only. Tints the entity's model (and optionally matching blocks in range) with a color, visible through walls to the ability holder only. SettingsExampleIn a power <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>mode</code><code>self</code><code>others</code>Whether this ability highlights the entity itself or lets you see other entities (self, others). Seeing others also needs "highlight_entities".✗SELF<code>color</code>ColorThe highlight color, defaulting to the team color for entities or white for blocks when unset.✗/<code>opacity</code>Float (0.0 ~ 1.0)How strong the tint is, from 0 (invisible) to 1 (fully opaque).✗0.1<code>distance</code>(Dynamic) ValueThe distance at which entities (in "others" mode) and enabled matching blocks are highlighted.✗0<code>style</code><code>tint</code><code>outline</code>How highlighted entities are drawn: tint fills the model with the color, outline draws the vanilla glow outline in the color (works on invisible entities too, and the opacity does not apply).✗TINT<code>highlight_entities</code>BooleanIf true, "others" mode also highlights entities within "distance" through walls. Off by default, so "others" only shows blocks. No effect in "self" mode.✗false<code>entities</code>Entity Type ID(s) / TagThe entity types to highlight when "highlight_entities" is true, given as explicit entity type IDs, a tag ("#namespace:tag"), or a mix of both. Left unset, every living entity in range is highlighted.✗/<code>highlight_blocks</code>BooleanIf true, also highlights every block matching "blocks" within "distance", through walls.✗false<code>blocks</code>Block ID(s) / TagThe blocks to highlight when "highlight_blocks" is true, given as explicit block IDs, a tag ("#namespace:tag"), or a mix of both.✗/<code>blocked_by</code>Block ID(s) / TagBlock types that still block line of sight for this effect, hiding anything behind them while everything else stays visible through walls.✗/<code>hostile_color</code>ColorHighlight color for hostile mobs (and neutral ones targeting the holder). When any of the four category colors is set, each highlighted entity uses the color of its category instead of "color"; categories without a color fall back to "color".✗/<code>ally_color</code>ColorHighlight color for allies: the holder's team members, owned pets, and entities allied through vanilla teams.✗/<code>neutral_color</code>ColorHighlight color for everything else that is not hostile, a player or an ally (animals, villagers, passive mobs).✗/<code>player_color</code>ColorHighlight color for players who are not allies.✗/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:highlight",
  "mode": "self",
  "color": "#FF0000",
  "opacity": 0.1,
  "distance": 16,
  "highlight_entities": true,
  "entities": [
    "minecraft:zombie",
    "#minecraft:skeletons"
  ],
  "highlight_blocks": true,
  "blocks": [
    "#minecraft:diamond_ores"
  ],
  "blocked_by": [
    "palladium:lead_block"
  ]
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>{
  "name": "Example Power",
  "icon": "minecraft:command_block",
  "abilities": {
    "highlight": {
      "type": "argon:highlight",
      "mode": "self",
      "color": "#FF0000",
      "opacity": 0.1,
      "distance": 16,
      "highlight_entities": true,
      "entities": [
        "minecraft:zombie",
        "#minecraft:skeletons"
      ],
      "highlight_blocks": true,
      "blocks": [
        "#minecraft:diamond_ores"
      ],
      "blocked_by": [
        "palladium:lead_block"
      ]
    }
  }
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:hunger_rate</code> While enabled, scales how fast a player's hunger drains and how much food and saturation they gain back. 100 is vanilla. 50 for drain means hunger drops half as fast, 50 for refill means food only restores half as much hunger and saturation (the fractions carry over, so two food points still add up). Values above 100 speed things up. Several enabled instances multiply their drain. SettingsExampleIn a power <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>drain_percent</code>IntegerPercent of the normal exhaustion the player takes from moving, jumping, fighting and regenerating. 0 stops hunger from draining.✗100<code>refill_percent</code>IntegerPercent of the normal food level and saturation the player gets back whenever either increases.✗100</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:hunger_rate",
  "drain_percent": 50,
  "properties": {
    "title": "Hunger Rate"
  }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>{
  "name": "Example Power",
  "icon": "minecraft:command_block",
  "abilities": {
    "hunger_rate": {
      "type": "argon:hunger_rate",
      "drain_percent": 50,
      "properties": {
        "title": "Hunger Rate"
      }
    }
  }
}
</code></pre>

