ID<code>argon:boxed_textured</code> Renders a beam as a stack of textured boxes like palladium's "textured" renderer, but each face has its own lighting normal. That fixes wrong or flickering brightness on side and bottom faces. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>texture</code>Texture ReferenceThe texture applied to each box segment.✓/<code>size</code>Float (&gt;= 0.0),Vector 2DThe cross-section size of each box segment, in blocks.✗0.0625<code>texture_length</code>FloatHow many blocks of beam one full copy of the texture covers, separate from "size". Keeps the arm's thickness fixed while you control how dense the texture is. Defaults to the average of "size"'s width and height.✗/<code>rotation</code>FloatA fixed rotation, in degrees, around the beam's own axis.✗0.0<code>rotation_speed</code>FloatDegrees per tick to continuously spin the boxes around the beam's own axis.✗0.0<code>render_type</code><code>minecraft:entity_translucent</code><code>minecraft:entity_cutout</code><code>minecraft:entity_cutout_cull</code><code>palladium:entity_translucent_cull</code><code>palladium:glow</code><code>palladium:glow_cull</code><code>argon:glow</code><code>argon:additive</code><code>argon:glow_no_depth_write</code><code>argon:glow_emission</code><code>argon:additive_emission</code>The render type to render the beam with.✗minecraft:entity_cutout<code>light_emission</code>IntegerThe light emission of the beam, from 0 to 15.✗0</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:boxed_textured",
  "texture": "namespace:textures/beams/example_beam.png",
  "size": 3
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:cone</code> Draws a beam as a cone from "start_size" to "end_size" blocks wide, with a soft glow, a brighter core and an optional scrolling texture. Pair it with the "cone" setting of argon:energy_beam. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>start_size</code>FloatHow wide the cone is where it starts, in blocks.✗0.15<code>end_size</code>FloatHow wide the cone is where it ends, in blocks.✗3.0<code>end_distance</code>FloatIf set, "end_size" is reached this many blocks from the start and the cone keeps that angle however long the beam is, so a beam stopped by a wall is narrower. Set it to the ability's max_distance. Without it, "end_size" is the width wherever the beam ends.✗/<code>glow</code>Cone LayerThe soft outer glow: its colour (hex), its opacity (0 turns it off) and its width compared with the whole cone's. Drawn as "glow_layers" nested cones.✗{ "color": "#FFFFFF", "opacity": 0.35, "scale": 1.0 }<code>core</code>Cone LayerThe brighter core inside the glow, set the same way.✗{ "color": "#FFFFFF", "opacity": 0.5, "scale": 0.45 }<code>glow_layers</code>IntegerHow many nested cones the glow is made of (1-8): more is softer and thicker toward the middle.✗3<code>texture</code>Cone TextureA texture over the cone's surface. Keys: texture*, color, opacity (0.6), scale (1), texture_length (2), around (2), scroll_speed (0.03), rotation_speed (0).✗/<code>blend</code><code>additive</code><code>translucent</code>How the cone is blended into the scene: additive glows brighter where layers overlap, translucent is a plain see-through surface.✗ADDITIVE<code>end_fade</code>FloatHow opaque the far end of the cone is compared to the start, 0 to 1. Lower fades the cone out along its length.✗0.2<code>segments</code>IntegerHow many flat sides the cone is drawn with (6-64): more is rounder.✗16</p> <p><p><code>glow</code> (Cone Layer)</p></p> <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>color</code>ColorThe color of the layer.✗/<code>opacity</code>Float (0.0 ~ 1.0)How opaque the layer is.✗/<code>scale</code>Float (0.01 ~ 4.0)The size of the layer relative to the cone.✗/</p> <p><p><code>texture</code> (Cone Texture)</p></p> <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>texture</code>IdentifierThe texture drawn on the cone.✓/<code>color</code>ColorThe tint of the texture.✗/<code>opacity</code>Float (0.0 ~ 1.0)How opaque the texture is.✗/<code>scale</code>Float (0.01 ~ 4.0)The size of the texture relative to the cone.✗/<code>texture_length</code>Float (0.05 ~ 64.0)How many blocks one repeat of the texture covers.✗/<code>around</code>Integer (1 ~ 16)How many times the texture repeats around the cone.✗/<code>scroll_speed</code>FloatHow fast the texture scrolls along the cone.✗/<code>rotation_speed</code>FloatHow fast the texture turns around the cone.✗/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:cone",
  "end_distance": 20
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:glow_laser</code> Palladium's laser beam with brightness settings for its glow and core. Below 1 fades the part, above 1 stacks extra draws for a stronger glow (up to 8). Both are values, so molang can animate them. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>render_settings</code>Laser Renderer SettingsThe render settings for the laser, exactly as for palladium:laser.✓/<code>glow_intensity</code>(Dynamic) ValueHow bright the glow is. 1 is the laser as Palladium draws it.✗1<code>core_intensity</code>(Dynamic) ValueHow bright the core is. 1 is the laser as Palladium draws it.✗1</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:glow_laser",
  "render_settings": {
    "bloom": 0
  },
  "glow_intensity": 2,
  "core_intensity": 1
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:ring</code> Draws a flat textured disc around the start of the beam, as wide as the beam is long, with bands that travel outward like shockwave rings. Only the beam's length matters, so it suits argon:anchored_beam, whose animation_timer fades it in and out. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>texture</code>IdentifierThe ring texture. Its U axis runs around the ring and its V axis outward.✓/<code>color</code>ColorA color multiplied with the texture.✗#FFFFFF<code>opacity</code>FloatHow opaque the ring is, 0 to 1.✗0.8<code>inner_radius</code>FloatRadius of the empty hole in the middle, in blocks.✗0.3<code>texture_length</code>FloatHow many blocks outward one repeat of the texture covers.✗4.0<code>around</code>IntegerHow many times the texture repeats around the ring.✗3<code>scroll_speed</code>FloatHow many texture repeats the bands travel outward per tick; negative pulls them inward.✗0.25<code>rotation_speed</code>FloatHow many degrees per tick the ring spins.✗0.0<code>twist</code>FloatHow many degrees the ring is twisted between its inner and outer edge, which curves the bands into spirals.✗0.0<code>end_fade</code>FloatHow opaque the outer edge is compared with the inner one, 0 to 1.✗0.0<code>height</code>FloatHow far above the start of the beam the ring is drawn, in blocks.✗0.05<code>blend</code><code>additive</code><code>translucent</code>How the ring is blended into the scene.✗TRANSLUCENT<code>segments</code>IntegerHow many flat sides the ring is drawn with (8-96).✗48<code>face_beam</code>BooleanIf true, the ring faces along the beam instead of lying flat, so it works as a circle in front of a hand.✗false</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:ring",
  "texture": "example:textures/beams/ring.png"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:segmented_textured</code> Renders a beam as flat textured layers around one axis that can tile, spin and scroll. Can sag near the origin and tighten over "sag_duration". Good for drill or corkscrew beams. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>textures</code>Beam Texture Layer[]The texture layers to render simultaneously around the beam axis, each either a sweeping ribbon ("facing": "radial", default) or a stack of flat cards facing down the beam ("along_beam").✓/<code>render_type</code><code>minecraft:entity_translucent</code><code>minecraft:entity_cutout</code><code>minecraft:entity_cutout_cull</code><code>palladium:entity_translucent_cull</code><code>palladium:glow</code><code>palladium:glow_cull</code><code>argon:glow</code><code>argon:additive</code><code>argon:glow_no_depth_write</code><code>argon:glow_emission</code><code>argon:additive_emission</code>The render type to render the beam with.✗minecraft:entity_translucent<code>light_emission</code>IntegerThe light emission of the beam, from 0 to 15.✗0<code>sag_amplitude</code>FloatHow far, in blocks, the beam bows sideways from a straight line when loose, at its most bowed point near the origin. Both ends always stay pinned to their anchors. 0 (default) keeps the beam perfectly straight.✗0.0<code>sag_waves</code>FloatHow many full side-to-side wave cycles the sag has along the beam, fading from full strength at the origin to none at the target. Only matters when "sag_amplitude" is above 0.✗2.0<code>sag_duration</code>Integer,Time String ("40s", "2m", etc.)Ticks for the beam to go from loose to taut after it starts being drawn for an entity. It starts loose again after a fresh attach (e.g. a new swing or grapple). Only matters when "sag_amplitude" is above 0.✗10<code>tint</code>Item TintTints every texture layer by the color a registered source reads off an item in "slot" (first match wins). "source" is an id an addon registered; with no match it stays white. Omit for no tint.✗/</p> <p><p><code>textures[]</code> (Beam Texture Layer)</p></p> <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>texture</code>Texture Reference,Dynamic TextureThe texture of the layer.✓/<code>size</code>Float (&gt;= 0.0),Vector 2DThe size of the layer, as one number or width and height.✗/<code>spacing</code>Float (&gt;= 0.0)The gap between repeats along the beam.✗/<code>rotation</code>Float (0.0 ~ 360.0)The starting rotation in degrees.✗/<code>rotation_speed</code>Float (&gt;= 0.0)How fast the layer rotates.✗/<code>motion_speed</code>FloatHow fast the layer moves along the beam.✗/<code>facing</code><code>radial</code><code>along_beam</code>Whether the layer faces outward from the beam or along it.✗/<code>pulse</code>PulseMakes the layer pulse in size.✗/</p> <p><p><code>tint</code> (Item Tint)</p></p> <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>slot</code><code>mainhand</code><code>offhand</code><code>head</code><code>chest</code><code>legs</code><code>feet</code><code>body</code><code>curios:head</code><code>curios:necklace</code>The slot of the item that is tinted.✓/<code>source</code>IdentifierThe tint source used.✓/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:segmented_textured",
  "textures": [
    {
      "texture": "namespace:textures/beams/example_beam.png",
      "size": 2,
      "spacing": 1,
      "rotation_speed": 20,
      "pulse": {
        "scale": 0.1,
        "frequency": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "texture": "namespace:textures/beams/example_beam.png",
      "size": 2,
      "spacing": 4,
      "rotation_speed": 10,
      "motion_speed": 8,
      "facing": "along_beam"
    }
  ],
  "sag_amplitude": 0.35
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:ability_bar_active</code> True while this ability sits in a slot of the given argon:ability_bar. Uses the crouch variant of the slot if it has one and the entity is crouching. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>ability_bar</code>StringThe argon:ability_bar ability to check, e.g. "fsang:marvel/mutant/reality_warping#my_bar". Left out, any bar of any power counts.✗</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:ability_bar_active",
  "ability_bar": "argon:example#my_bar"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:ability_bar_active_crouch</code> True while the entity is crouching and this ability sits in a crouch slot of the given argon:ability_bar. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>ability_bar</code>StringThe argon:ability_bar ability to check, e.g. "fsang:marvel/mutant/reality_warping#my_bar". Left out, any bar of any power counts.✗</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:ability_bar_active_crouch",
  "ability_bar": "argon:example#my_bar"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:ability_enabled_activation</code> Like the vanilla "Ability enabled" condition, but sticky. Once the ability is seen enabled, it keeps passing for the given time after it stops. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>ability</code>Ability ReferenceThe ability to watch for being enabled.✓/<code>duration</code>Integer,Time String ("40s", "2m", etc.)How long this stays true after the referenced ability was last seen enabled.✓/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:ability_enabled_activation",
  "ability": "example:power#some_ability",
  "duration": 100
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:ability_type_enabled_activation</code> Like the vanilla "Ability type enabled" condition, but sticky. Once any ability of "ability_type" is seen enabled, it keeps passing for the given time after they stop. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>ability_type</code>Ability Type ID(s) / TagThe ability type(s) to watch for being enabled.✓/<code>duration</code>Integer,Time String ("40s", "2m", etc.)How long this stays true after an ability of a matching type was last seen enabled.✓/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:ability_type_enabled_activation",
  "ability_type": [
    "argon:effect"
  ],
  "duration": "5s"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:advancement</code> Checks if the user has a given advancement. Server only, so it is always false on the client. Don't use it in a render_layer's conditions. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>advancement</code>IdentifierThe id of the advancement the user must have completed.✓/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:advancement",
  "advancement": "minecraft:story/mine_diamond"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:biome</code> Checks if the user is currently standing in one of the specified biomes. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>biomes</code>Biome ID(s) / TagThe biome(s) to check for, as explicit biome IDs, a tag ("#namespace:tag"), or a mix of both.✓/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:biome",
  "biomes": [
    "minecraft:desert",
    "#minecraft:is_nether"
  ]
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:biome_swap_area</code> Checks if the user stands inside an active argon:biome_swap area, optionally one made with a specific biome. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>biome</code>IdentifierA biome the area must have been made with to count. Otherwise any active area counts.✗/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:biome_swap_area",
  "biome": "minecraft:desert"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:block_below</code> Checks if the block directly beneath the user's feet matches "blocks". Fluids count as blocks, so "minecraft:water" is true while standing on water, for example with fluid walking. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>blocks</code>Block ID(s) / TagThe block(s) to look for, as explicit block IDs, a tag ("#namespace:tag"), or a mix of both.✓/<code>distance</code>DoubleHow many blocks below the feet are searched. 0.5 only checks the block the user is standing on, a larger value also passes while hovering above a matching block.✗0.5<code>require_ground</code>BooleanIf true, the user also has to be on the ground (or on a fluid they can walk on).✗false</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:block_below",
  "require_ground": true,
  "blocks": [
    "#minecraft:sand"
  ]
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:carriable</code> True while you look at something palladium:carry could pick up: any other living entity within "range" blocks. Handy for showing a prompt only when there is a target. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>range</code>DoubleHow far along the look direction to search, in blocks. Left out, it uses the referenced argon:carry ability's range, or 3 blocks without one.✗/<code>ability</code>Ability ReferenceAn argon:carry ability ("namespace:power#ability") to check for. A target only counts if that ability could grab it: clear line of sight, not already carried, allowed by allow_mobs / allow_players. Left out, Palladium's own carry rules apply.✗/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:carriable",
  "range": 2
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:carried</code> True while the entity is held by someone's "argon:carry" ability (the carrier's side is "argon:carrying"). Works for any living entity that can be carried. Example <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:carried"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:carrying</code> True while the entity holds something with argon:carry. Unlike palladium:carrying, which only sees palladium:carry, this works for any living carrier. Example <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:carrying"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:danger_sense_active</code> Checks if the user has an enabled argon:danger_sense ability that is detecting at least one danger: a nearby hostile mob or a player who recently hurt the user. Example <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:danger_sense_active"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:danger_sense_count</code> Checks how many dangers the user's enabled argon:danger_sense ability is detecting (the largest count if there are several). Useful for triggering an overload once enough threats are around. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>min</code>IntegerThe fewest detected dangers that still pass.✗1<code>max</code>IntegerThe most detected dangers that still pass.✗2147483647</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:danger_sense_count",
  "min": 8
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:dodge_direction</code> Checks if the referenced dodge ability is showing its dodge pose and the dodge went mostly in the given direction relative to where the entity faces. Diagonals count as whichever axis is stronger. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>ability</code>Ability ReferenceThe dodge ability to check.✓/<code>direction</code><code>forward</code><code>back</code><code>left</code><code>right</code>The direction to check.✓/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:dodge_direction",
  "ability": "example:power#dodge",
  "direction": "forward"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:dodging</code> Checks if the referenced dodge ability is actively showing its dodge pose right now. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>ability</code>Ability ReferenceThe dodge ability to check.✓/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:dodging",
  "ability": "example:power#dodge"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:durability</code> Checks if the item worn or held in the given slot has durability left, as a percentage, between min and max. Fails for an empty slot or an item that can't be damaged. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>slot</code><code>mainhand</code><code>offhand</code><code>head</code><code>chest</code><code>legs</code><code>feet</code><code>body</code><code>curios:head</code><code>curios:necklace</code>Which slot to check, e.g. an armor slot.✗chest<code>min</code>DoubleThe minimum remaining durability percentage required (0-100).✗0.0<code>max</code>DoubleThe maximum remaining durability percentage allowed (0-100).✗100.0</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:durability",
  "max": 20
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:falling</code> Checks if the user is in the air and moving downward, so not on the ground and not rising. Example <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:falling"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:grappling</code> Checks if the referenced grapple ability (argon:grapple or palladium:grapple) is currently grappled to a block, an entity or either, depending on the mode. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>ability</code>Ability ReferenceThe grapple ability to check (argon:grapple or palladium:grapple).✓/<code>mode</code><code>block</code><code>entity</code><code>either</code>Which kind of grapple to check for.✗EITHER</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:grappling",
  "ability": "example:power#grapple"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:ground_distance</code> Checks if the user is on the ground or within max_distance blocks of solid ground directly below. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>max_distance</code>DoubleThe maximum distance, in blocks, to the ground below the user.✓/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:ground_distance",
  "max_distance": 3
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:has_energy</code> Checks if the item in the given slot has stored energy between min and max. Infinite and creative energy items always pass. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>slot</code><code>mainhand</code><code>offhand</code><code>head</code><code>chest</code><code>legs</code><code>feet</code><code>body</code><code>curios:head</code><code>curios:necklace</code>Which slot to look for the energy-storing item in.✗curios:hands<code>min</code>IntegerThe minimum stored energy required.✗0<code>max</code>IntegerThe maximum stored energy allowed.✗2147483647</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:has_energy",
  "min": 1000,
  "max": 5000
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:has_fluid</code> Checks whether the fluid item in the given slot has a stored fluid amount between min and max. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>slot</code><code>mainhand</code><code>offhand</code><code>head</code><code>chest</code><code>legs</code><code>feet</code><code>body</code><code>curios:head</code><code>curios:necklace</code>Which slot to look for the fluid-storing item in.✗curios:hands<code>min</code>IntegerThe minimum stored fluid amount required.✗0<code>max</code>IntegerThe maximum stored fluid amount allowed.✗2147483647</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:has_fluid",
  "min": 100,
  "max": 500
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:has_input</code> Checks if the user is holding a movement key (forward, back, left or right). Unlike palladium:has_movement_input it also works while a world is loading and in single player's built-in server. Example <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:has_input"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:has_item</code> Checks if the user has at least one matching item anywhere on them: inventory, worn equipment and Curios slots where they apply. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>items</code>Item ID(s) / TagThe item(s) to search for, given as explicit item IDs, a tag ("#namespace:tag"), or a mix of both.✓/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:has_item",
  "items": [
    "minecraft:totem_of_undying"
  ]
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:has_power</code> Checks if the user has a matching power. "include" (default) needs at least one present, "exclude" needs all of them absent. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>power</code>Power ID(s) / TagThe power(s) to check for, given as explicit power IDs, a tag ("#namespace:tag"), or a mix of both.✓/<code>mode</code><code>include</code><code>exclude</code>Whether a matching power must be present ("include") or absent ("exclude").✗INCLUDE</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:has_power",
  "power": "#palladium:arachnid"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:has_stored_equipment</code> Checks if the store_equipment ability has anything currently stored under the given ID. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>id</code>StringThe ID to check.✓/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:has_stored_equipment",
  "id": "ranger_armor"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:has_stored_item</code> Checks if at least one matching item is stored in any slot of the referenced argon:item_storage ability. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>item_storage</code>StringThe argon:item_storage ability whose slots to check, e.g. "fsang:example#my_storage".✓/<code>items</code>Item ID(s) / TagThe item(s) to search for among the stored slots, given as explicit item IDs, a tag ("#namespace:tag"), or a mix of both.✓/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:has_stored_item",
  "item_storage": "fsang:example#my_storage",
  "items": [
    "minecraft:totem_of_undying"
  ]
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:has_suit_modification</code> Checks if the entity wears a complete Palladium suit with the given Tech Table modification installed on every piece. Can be limited to one suit set. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>modification</code>StringThe id of the suit modification (the file name under data//argon/suit_modification/).✓/<code>suit</code>StringThe id of a Palladium suit set the worn suit has to be; any suit when left out.✗<code>slot</code><code>mainhand</code><code>offhand</code><code>head</code><code>chest</code><code>legs</code><code>feet</code><code>body</code><code>curios:head</code><code>curios:necklace</code>Check the item worn or held in this slot instead of a suit (for modules installed on a device item in the Modify tab).✗chest<code>min_level</code>IntegerFor modules that can be installed several times: the lowest level that counts.✗1 <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:has_suit_modification",
  "modification": "example:reserve_battery"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:has_tech_slot_item</code> True while the item in the given player slot has something in one of its Tech Table Customize slots. "tech_slot" limits it to one slot ID, "items" to certain items or item tags ("#namespace:tag"). SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>slot</code><code>mainhand</code><code>offhand</code><code>head</code><code>chest</code><code>legs</code><code>feet</code><code>body</code><code>curios:head</code><code>curios:necklace</code>The player slot holding the customized item.✓/<code>tech_slot</code>IdentifierOnly check the Customize slot with this ID.✗/<code>items</code>String, String[]Only count these items or item tags.✗/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:has_tech_slot_item",
  "slot": "chest",
  "tech_slot": "example:core"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:health</code> Checks if the user's current health, as a percentage of their current max health (with max health modifiers), is between min and max (inclusive). SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>min</code>DoubleThe minimum health percentage required (0-100).✗0.0<code>max</code>DoubleThe maximum health percentage allowed (0-100).✗100.0</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:health",
  "max": 25
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:is_female</code> True if the wearer picked "Female" in the argon:body customization. A shorthand for palladium:customization_selected, handy for limiting a body overlay to one chestplate. Example <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:is_female"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:jumping</code> Checks if the user is in the air and moving upward, not standing on the ground or falling. Example <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:jumping"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:karma_alignment</code> True while the player's alignment is the given one. Use positive, neutral or negative for a whole side, or an alignment id such as hero or mastermind. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>alignment</code><code>positive</code><code>neutral</code><code>negative</code><code>mastermind</code><code>villain</code><code>thug</code><code>lackey</code><code>neutral</code><code>vigilante</code><code>protector</code><code>hero</code><code>legend</code>The alignment id or side to check.✓/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:karma_alignment",
  "alignment": "mastermind"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:karma_range</code> True while the player's karma is within the inclusive range. Karma goes from -5000 to 5000 by default. Use a maximum of -501 for "below -500". SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>minimum</code>IntegerThe lowest karma that passes, inclusive.✗-2147483648<code>maximum</code>IntegerThe highest karma that passes, inclusive.✗2147483647</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:karma_range",
  "minimum": -5000,
  "maximum": -501
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:key_press_on_block</code> True while the referenced ability's key is held and you look at a block matching "blocks" within "range". Handy for climbing, which needs both the key and a climbable surface. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>ability</code>Ability ReferenceThe ability whose key_bind key-press state to check.✓/<code>blocks</code>Block ID(s) / TagThe block(s) the user must be looking at, as explicit block IDs, a tag ("#namespace:tag"), or a mix of both.✓/<code>range</code>DoubleHow far, in blocks, the look-direction raycast reaches.✓/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:key_press_on_block",
  "ability": "example:power#wall_crawling",
  "range": 5,
  "blocks": [
    "#minecraft:walls"
  ]
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:looking_at_entity</code> True while the entity is looking at another entity within range. The entity can be limited to certain types, a single type or a tag. Blocks in the way hide entities behind them unless through_blocks is set. SettingsExample 1Example 2 <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>entity_types</code>Entity Type ID(s) / TagThe entity types that count, as one entity type ID, a tag ("#namespace:tag"), or a list mixing both. Left out, any entity counts.✗/<code>range</code>Double (0.1 ~ 512.0)How far along the look direction to search, in blocks.✓/<code>through_blocks</code>BooleanIf true, entities behind blocks still count. If false, a block in the way hides them.✗false</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:looking_at_entity",
  "range": 8
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:looking_at_entity",
  "range": 12,
  "entity_types": [
    "minecraft:villager",
    "#minecraft:skeletons"
  ]
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:main_hand</code> Checks the player's main hand setting (left or right handed), not which hand holds an item. Always false for non-players. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>hand</code><code>none</code><code>main_arm</code><code>off_arm</code><code>right_arm</code><code>left_arm</code><code>both</code>The main hand to check for.✓/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:main_hand",
  "hand": "right"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:marked_target</code> Checks if the entity has a living, unexpired target mark from argon:target_mark. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>moving_toward</code>BooleanIf true, the entity must also be moving toward the marked target.✗false<code>max_distance</code>DoubleIf above 0, the marked target must be within this many blocks.✗0.0</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:marked_target",
  "moving_toward": true
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:movement_speed</code> True while the entity's actual speed, measured from how far it moved since the previous tick, is within min and max (inclusive), in blocks per second. Works on the client too, so it can drive trails and other visuals. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>min</code>DoubleThe lowest speed in blocks per second that passes.✗0.0<code>max</code>DoubleThe highest speed in blocks per second that passes. Unset means no upper limit.✗/<code>horizontal_only</code>BooleanIf true, vertical movement is ignored.✗false</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:movement_speed",
  "min": 12,
  "horizontal_only": true
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:movement_speed_percent</code> True while the referenced movement_speed_adjuster dial is within "min" and "max" (inclusive). Handy for gating a speed tier on the dial being turned up. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>ability</code>Ability ReferenceThe movement_speed_adjuster ability whose dial to check.✓/<code>min</code>IntegerThe lowest dial value this still passes for.✗-2147483648<code>max</code>IntegerThe highest dial value this still passes for.✗2147483647</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:movement_speed_percent",
  "ability": "example:power#movement_speed_adjuster",
  "min": 50
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:nearby_block</code> Checks if at least one block matching "blocks" is within "radius" of the user. Scans every block in range on each check. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>blocks</code>Block ID(s) / TagThe block(s) to search for, as explicit block IDs, a tag ("#namespace:tag"), or a mix of both.✓/<code>radius</code>DoubleHow far from the user to search.✓/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:nearby_block",
  "radius": 8,
  "blocks": [
    "#minecraft:diamond_ores"
  ]
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:nearby_entities</code> Checks if at least one other entity is within range, optionally only living or only non-living ones, and/or ones that do or don't have specific powers. SettingsExample 1Example 2 <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>entity_filter</code><code>living</code><code>non_living</code><code>all</code>Which kinds of entities count: 'living' for players/mobs, 'non_living' for everything else.✗ALL<code>radius</code>DoubleHow far from the entity to search.✓/<code>powers</code>Power Requirement,Power Requirement[]Restricts which entities count based on the powers they hold. Each entry lists a "power" (an explicit power id, a power tag ("#namespace:tag"), or a mix of both) and an optional "mode" - "include" (default) requires at least one matching power to be present, "exclude" requires all of them to be absent; an entity only counts if it satisfies every entry. Non-living entities never satisfy an "include" entry. Leave unset (the default) to not filter by power at all.✗/</p> <p><p><code>powers</code> (Power Requirement)</p></p> <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>power</code>Power ID(s) / TagThe power or powers the entity has to have.✓/<code>mode</code><code>include</code><code>exclude</code>Whether the entity must have the power (include) or must not have it (exclude).✗include</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:nearby_entities",
  "entity_filter": "living",
  "radius": 8
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:nearby_entities",
  "entity_filter": "living",
  "radius": 8,
  "powers": [
    {
      "power": "example:ally_power",
      "mode": "include"
    },
    {
      "power": "example:hostile_power",
      "mode": "exclude"
    }
  ]
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:perspective</code> Checks if the owning player is in first or third person, synced from the client to the server. Defaults to first person and is always false for non-players. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>first_person</code>BooleanTrue to check for first person, false to check for third person (front or back).✓/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:perspective",
  "first_person": true
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:possessing</code> Passes while the entity is possessing another entity with an argon:possess ability. Without an ability set, any possess ability the entity has counts. SettingsExample 1Example 2 <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>ability</code>Ability ReferenceOnly check this possess ability. Any possess ability counts if unset.✗/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:possessing"
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:possessing",
  "ability": "example:power#possess"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:reacted</code> Checks if the referenced argon:damage_reaction ability reacted to a hit within the given time, optionally to one specific reaction. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>ability</code>Ability ReferenceThe damage reaction ability to check.✓/<code>within</code>Integer,Time String ("40s", "2m", etc.)How recently the reaction must have happened.✗20<code>reaction</code>StringOnly count the reaction with this id.✗/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:reacted",
  "ability": "example:power#reaction"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:recently_attacked</code> Checks if the entity dealt damage to anything (melee, ranged or from an ability) within the given time. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>within</code>Integer,Time String ("40s", "2m", etc.)How recently the entity must have last attacked something for this to pass.✓/<code>or_hurt_by_hostile</code>BooleanIf true, the condition also passes when a hostile mob damaged the entity within that time.✗false</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:recently_attacked",
  "within": 60
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:saturation</code> Checks if the user's saturation level is at least the given amount. Always false for non-players. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>amount</code>FloatThe minimum saturation level required.✓/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:saturation",
  "amount": 4
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:sky_access</code> Passes while nothing solid is above the user's head, so they can see the open sky. Combine with a not condition to detect being underground or under a roof. Example <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:sky_access"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:targeted_by_mob</code> Checks if at least one mob within range has the user as its AI attack target. Scans nearby mobs' targets rather than a global registry. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>range</code>DoubleHow far out to search for mobs that might be targeting the user.✓/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:targeted_by_mob",
  "range": 24
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:tech_toggle</code> True while a Tech Table Customize button is switched on for the item in the given player slot. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>slot</code><code>mainhand</code><code>offhand</code><code>head</code><code>chest</code><code>legs</code><code>feet</code><code>body</code><code>curios:head</code><code>curios:necklace</code>The player slot holding the customized item.✓/<code>toggle</code>IdentifierThe ID of the button.✓/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:tech_toggle",
  "slot": "chest",
  "toggle": "example:overdrive"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:time_of_day</code> Checks if the overworld's time of day is between min and max inclusive. Wraps around midnight if max is smaller than min. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>min</code>IntegerThe start of the time range, in ticks (0 = dawn, 6000 = midday, 12000 = dusk, 18000 = midnight).✓/<code>max</code>IntegerThe end of the time range, in ticks, wrapping around midnight if smaller than min.✓/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:time_of_day",
  "min": 13000,
  "max": 23000
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:took_off</code> True for a short time after an "argon:charged_takeoff" launched the entity. Use it to hand the launch over to a flight ability, e.g. as the enabling of an "argon:force_start_flight". SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>within</code>Integer,Time String ("40s", "2m", etc.)How long after the launch this stays true.✗10</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:took_off"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:wall_crawling_active</code> Checks if the user has an enabled argon:wall_crawling ability that is attached to a wall or ceiling. Only true while on a surface, not whenever the ability is enabled. Example <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:wall_crawling_active"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:weather</code> Passes while the world the entity is in has the given weather. Only the real world weather counts; local storms made by abilities do not change it. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>weather</code><code>clear</code><code>raining</code><code>thundering</code>The weather to test for. Raining also passes during a thunderstorm.✗thundering</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:weather"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:y_level</code> Checks if the user is currently at or above a specified Y level. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>min_y</code>DoubleThe minimum Y coordinate the user must be at or above.✓/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:y_level",
  "min_y": 64
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:armor_or_skin</code> For models on the body, like claws. Uses the worn suit's texture where an active layer from "model_layers" draws over the given region, otherwise the skin. Match the region to the model's UVs. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>regions</code>Integer[][]The areas of the texture sheet to check, each as [u, v, width, height] in pixels (a 64x64 sheet). Should cover the pixels your model samples. Defaults to the bottom of the left arm: [[40, 48, 4, 4]].✗[ [ 40, 48, 4, 4 ] ]<code>min_coverage</code>FloatHow much of the region must be drawn (0 to 1) for the suit to count as covering it. 1 means every pixel.✗1.0<code>model_layers</code>string[]The model layer names that count as a suit, like "suit_chestplate" in "palladium:player#suit_chestplate".✗[ "suit_chestplate" ]</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:armor_or_skin"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:chest_armor</code> Uses the texture of the player's active "suit_chestplate" render layer, so an overlay like argon:female_chest always matches the worn suit. Shows the missing texture if no such layer is active. Example <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:chest_armor"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:mouth_selection</code> Like palladium:eye_selection, but for the pixels picked on the eye screen's "Mouth" tab. A pixel is an eye or a mouth, never both. Needs the argon:customization/mouth_selection flag. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>color</code>ColorThe color the mouth pixels are drawn in.✗-1</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:mouth_selection",
  "color": "#ff8080"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:eye_selection_secondary</code> Like palladium:eye_selection, but for the pixels picked on the eye screen's "Secondary" tab, so a pack can color a second set on its own. Needs the argon:customization/secondary_eye_selection flag. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>color</code>ColorThe color the secondary pixels are drawn in.✗-1</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:eye_selection_secondary",
  "color": "#00ffff"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:forced_flight</code> Flight matching Palladium's default movement and animation, except the entity can never land or toggle out. It only stops when the granting ability is disabled. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>speed</code>FloatThe normal flight speed.✓/<code>sprint_speed</code>FloatThe sprinting flight speed, defaulting to the normal speed when unset.✗/<code>screen_shake</code>Screen ShakeCamera shake while sprinting in flight. Only used when a sprint_speed is set.✗/<code>propelled_sound</code>IdentifierLooping sound played while fast-flying (sprinting), its volume scaling with flight speed.✗/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:forced_flight",
  "speed": 1,
  "sprint_speed": 2
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:add_power</code> Gives the item a Palladium power while it is in one of the listed slots. The power is taken away when the function stops applying. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>power</code>IdentifierThe power to give.✓/<code>slots</code>String, String[]Player slots such as mainhand, chest or curios:body.✗The slots the item already gives powers in, else its equipment slot, else mainhand<code>pieces</code>String[]On a suit module, the armor pieces it goes on: head, chest, legs, feet.✗See above</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:add_power",
  "power": "example:jet_boots",
  "slots": "feet"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:copy_component</code> Copies a data component from the inserted item to the base item, for example a dye color. Only does something in a Customize slot, where there is an inserted item. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>component</code>IdentifierThe component written on the base item.✓/<code>from</code>IdentifierThe component read from the inserted item.✗The value of component<code>pieces</code>String[]On a suit module, the armor pieces it goes on: head, chest, legs, feet.✗See above</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:copy_component",
  "component": "minecraft:dyed_color"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:energy_capacity</code> Modules only. Raises the energy capacity of an item that stores energy. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>per_level</code>IntegerCapacity added for each level.✓/<code>max</code>IntegerThe highest capacity the effect can reach.✓/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:energy_capacity",
  "per_level": 5000,
  "max": 50000
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:fluid_capacity</code> Modules only. Raises the capacity of an item that stores fluid, up to the item's own maximum. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>per_level</code>IntegerCapacity in mB added for each level.✓/</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:fluid_capacity",
  "per_level": 500
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:integer_component</code> Modules only. Sets a whole number data component to the level multiplied by per_level. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>component</code>IdentifierThe component to set.✓/<code>per_level</code>IntegerAmount for each level.✗1</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:integer_component",
  "component": "example:charges",
  "per_level": 2
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:item_id_to_component</code> Writes the inserted item's name (the part after the namespace) to a text component. Nothing is written if the name does not start with strip_prefix. Only does something in a Customize slot. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>component</code>IdentifierA text data component.✓/<code>strip_prefix</code>StringText removed from the start of the name.✗Empty<code>pieces</code>String[]On a suit module, the armor pieces it goes on: head, chest, legs, feet.✗See above</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:item_id_to_component",
  "component": "example:casing",
  "strip_prefix": "casing_"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:set_component</code> Sets any data component on the item. When the function stops applying, the component goes back to the item's default. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>component</code>IdentifierThe component to set.✓/<code>value</code>AnyThe value, written the way the component is written in an item file.✓/<code>clears</code>Identifier[]Other components reset along with this one.✗[]<code>pieces</code>String[]On a suit module, the armor pieces it goes on: head, chest, legs, feet.✗See above</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:set_component",
  "component": "minecraft:max_damage",
  "value": 500
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:tooltip</code> Adds a line to the item's tooltip. SettingsExample <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>text</code>ComponentThe text to show.✓/<code>pieces</code>String[]On a suit module, the armor pieces it goes on: head, chest, legs, feet.✗See above</p> <pre><code>{
  "type": "argon:tooltip",
  "text": "Jet boots installed"
}
</code></pre>

ID<code>argon:cloth</code> Renders a simulated cloth grid (a cape by default) that flows, bends and trails behind the wearer instead of turning as one flat plane. SettingsExample 1Example 2Example 3Example 4Example 5 <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>attachment</code><code>back</code><code>left_arm</code><code>right_arm</code><code>waist</code>Which part of the cloth grid is pinned to the wearer: "back" (cape), "left_arm" or "right_arm" (draped along an arm), or "waist" (skirt or kilt, a box-shaped loop around the hips).✗back<code>material</code>Identifier,Inline Material ObjectA registered material id (e.g. "argon:heavy") or an inline object overriding individual physics properties.✗argon:standard<code>size</code>Vector 2D[length, width] of the cloth at rest, in blocks. For a "waist" attachment, "width" is the half-width of the left and right sides of the box. "depth" sets the front and back.✗[ 1.8, 1 ]<code>segments</code>Vector 2D[segments_x, segments_y]: simulation columns/rows. More = smoother bending at a performance cost.✗[ 4, 8 ]<code>wrap</code>Float"back" attachment only. Curves the pinned edge forward so it hugs the wearer's sides, turning a flat cape into a wrap-around cloak. 0 is flat.✗0.0<code>depth</code>Float"waist" attachment only. Half-depth of the front and back sides, in blocks. Usually smaller than "width", since players are thinner front to back. Defaults to "width".✗/<code>pitch_influence</code>Float"waist" attachment only. While crouching, the garment follows the body's pitch as one piece. This scales how much, 0 ignores it and 1 follows fully.✗0.6<code>hem_pitch_fraction</code>FloatCurrently unused. Kept so existing configs that set it still load.✗0.25<code>attach</code>Vector 3D[x, y, z] offset of the attach point from the neck, in wearer space. Y=0 is neck height and higher Y goes down. A cape needs a positive Z to sit behind. For "waist" use X=0, Z=0 and Y about 0.75-0.9.✗[ 0, 0.1, 0.4 ]<code>taper</code>Float"back" attachment only. Width at the shoulders as a fraction of the width at the hem, 0.05 to 1. Below 1 the cloth is a trapezoid that flares out to "width" at the hem. 1 is a plain rectangle.✗1.0<code>aerodynamics</code>Cloth AerodynamicsHow the cloth reacts to the wearer's real speed, not the selected flight level. Each part is documented in the tables below.✗{}<code>texture</code>Texture Reference,Dynamic TextureThe texture for the cloth. UVs stay stable across the grid however the cloth deforms.✓/<code>render_type</code><code>minecraft:entity_translucent</code><code>minecraft:entity_cutout</code><code>minecraft:entity_cutout_cull</code><code>palladium:entity_translucent_cull</code><code>palladium:glow</code><code>palladium:glow_cull</code><code>argon:glow</code><code>argon:additive</code><code>argon:glow_no_depth_write</code><code>argon:glow_emission</code><code>argon:additive_emission</code>The render type to draw the cloth with.✗minecraft:entity_translucent<code>transformation</code>Identifier,Identifier[]Transformation ID(s). The layer only shows while one of these transformations is shown, on the transformed model. Without this field the layer is hidden while a transformation model is shown. Layers inside a compound layer without their own value use the one of the compound.✗/<code>shrink_player_parts</code><code>cape</code><code>jacket</code><code>left_sleeve</code><code>right_sleeve</code><code>left_pants_leg</code><code>right_pants_leg</code><code>hat</code>Skin overlay part(s) that are scaled down slightly while this layer is visible, so they don't stick out of the layer's model. "cape" has no effect.✗/</p> <p><p><code>aerodynamics</code> (Cloth Aerodynamics)</p></p> <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>airflow_strength</code>Float (0.0 ~ 4.0)How strongly the wearer's speed blows the cloth back.✗1.0<code>turn_inertia</code>Float (0.0 ~ 4.0)How much the cloth lags behind when the wearer turns.✗1.0<code>flight_stabilization</code>Float (0.0 ~ 1.0)Calms the swinging of the cloth at speed.✗0.6<code>flight_stiffness_multiplier</code>Float (1.0 ~ 3.0)Multiplies the stiffness of the cloth during flight.✗1.15<code>stiffness_response</code>Float (0.01 ~ 1.0)How quickly the stiffness follows the speed.✗0.1<code>body_hug</code>Float (0.0 ~ 1.0)How tightly a back cape is pulled against the body near top speed.✗0.75<code>hug_start</code>Float (0.0 ~ 0.99)How far through the speed range the hugging begins.✗0.6<code>limb_collision</code>Cloth Limb CollisionKeeps the cloth out of the wearer's body. Back capes only.✗/<code>narrowing</code>Cloth NarrowingNarrows the cloth as the wearer speeds up.✗/<code>flutter</code>Cloth FlutterMakes the cloth flutter at speed.✗/</p> <p><p><code>aerodynamics.limb_collision</code> (Cloth Limb Collision)</p></p> <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>enabled</code>BooleanWhether the cloth is kept out of the body shapes. Back capes only.✗true<code>torso</code>Cloth Torso ShapeThe torso shape the cloth stays out of.✗/<code>head</code>Cloth Head ShapeThe head shape the cloth stays out of.✗/<code>arms</code>Cloth Arm ShapeThe arm shape the cloth stays out of.✗/<code>legs</code>Cloth Leg ShapeThe leg shape the cloth stays out of.✗/</p> <p><p><code>aerodynamics.limb_collision.torso</code> (Cloth Torso Shape)</p></p> <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>width</code>Float (0.1 ~ 2.0)The width of the shape in blocks.✗0.5<code>depth</code>Float (0.05 ~ 1.0)The depth of the shape in blocks.✗0.25<code>length</code>Float (0.1 ~ 2.0)The length of the shape in blocks.✗1.1<code>radius</code>Float (0.0 ~ 0.6)How rounded the corners are, in blocks.✗0.1</p> <p><p><code>aerodynamics.limb_collision.head</code> (Cloth Head Shape)</p></p> <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>width</code>Float (0.1 ~ 2.0)The width of the shape in blocks.✗0.5<code>depth</code>Float (0.05 ~ 1.0)The depth of the shape in blocks.✗0.5<code>length</code>Float (0.1 ~ 2.0)The length of the shape in blocks.✗0.5<code>radius</code>Float (0.0 ~ 0.6)How rounded the corners are, in blocks.✗0.1</p> <p><p><code>aerodynamics.limb_collision.arms</code> (Cloth Arm Shape)</p></p> <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>radius</code>Float (0.05 ~ 1.0)The radius of the limb in blocks.✗0.3<code>length</code>Float (0.1 ~ 1.5)The length of the limb in blocks.✗0.8</p> <p><p><code>aerodynamics.limb_collision.legs</code> (Cloth Leg Shape)</p></p> <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>radius</code>Float (0.05 ~ 1.0)The radius of the limb in blocks.✗0.25<code>length</code>Float (0.1 ~ 1.5)The length of the limb in blocks.✗0.8</p> <p><p><code>aerodynamics.narrowing</code> (Cloth Narrowing)</p></p> <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>enabled</code>BooleanWhether the cloth narrows at speed. Not used by waist cloth.✗true<code>start_speed</code>Float (0.0 ~ 1000.0)The speed in blocks per second where narrowing starts when there is no flight.✗10.0<code>full_speed</code>Float (0.0 ~ 1000.0)The speed in blocks per second where narrowing is full when there is no flight.✗40.0<code>start_fraction</code>Float (0.0 ~ 2.0)The fraction of the wearer's top flight speed where narrowing starts.✗0.25<code>full_fraction</code>Float (0.0 ~ 2.0)The fraction of the wearer's top flight speed where narrowing is full.✗0.85<code>maximum</code>Float (0.0 ~ 0.9)The most the cloth narrows. The default is 0.35 when wrap is above 0 or for arms.✗0.6<code>response</code>Float (0.001 ~ 1.0)How quickly the cloth narrows.✗0.06<code>recovery</code>Float (0.001 ~ 1.0)How quickly the cloth widens again.✗0.04<code>distribution</code>Float (0.1 ~ 6.0)Higher values keep the top of the cloth wider.✗1.5<code>fold</code>Float (0.0 ~ 1.5)How much the cloth folds into pleats. 0 turns the pleats off.✗1.0<code>compression</code>Float (0.0 ~ 0.9)How much of the narrowing squeezes the cloth itself instead of folding it.✗0.5</p> <p><p><code>aerodynamics.flutter</code> (Cloth Flutter)</p></p> <p>KeyTypeDescriptionRequiredFallback<code>intensity</code>Float (0.0 ~ 5.0)How strong the fluttering is.✗1.0<code>frequency</code>Float (0.1 ~ 5.0)How fast the cloth flutters.✗1.0<code>speed_influence</code>Float (0.0 ~ 4.0)How much the wearer's speed adds to the fluttering.✗1.0<code>damping</code>Float (0.0 ~ 1.0)How quickly the fluttering dies down.✗0.6<code>edge_multiplier</code>Float (1.0 ~ 5.0)How much stronger the fluttering is at the free edge.✗1.5</p> <pre><code>{
